<h2>Start with compliance (not creativity)</h2>

<p>Before you write a single message, you need to understand the rules. In Australia, SMS marketing is governed by the Spam Act 2003 and enforced by the Australian Communications and Media Authority (ACMA). Similar legislation exists in most countries, including the TCPA in the United States, GDPR in Europe, and CASL in Canada. The specifics vary, but the principles are consistent everywhere.</p>

<p><strong>Explicit consent is required.</strong> You cannot send marketing SMS to someone who has not actively opted in. A customer giving you their phone number to book an appointment does not give you permission to send them promotional texts. You need a clear, separate opt-in. "Yes, I would like to receive marketing messages via SMS" is the kind of language you need, and you should keep a record of when and how each person opted in.</p>

<p><strong>Identify yourself in every message.</strong> The recipient must know who the message is from. Use your business name as the sender ID, and make sure it is recognisable. A text from an unknown number with no business name attached will get ignored or reported.</p>

<p><strong>Include an opt-out in every marketing message.</strong> "Reply STOP to opt out" is the standard approach. When someone opts out, honour it immediately. No delay, no confirmation message, no attempt to change their mind. One opt-out request means that contact is off your list, full stop.</p>

<p><strong>Understand the difference between transactional and marketing messages.</strong> Appointment reminders, booking confirmations, and delivery updates are transactional. They serve the customer's existing relationship with your business and have less restrictive rules. Promotional offers, sales announcements, and re-engagement campaigns are marketing messages. They require explicit opt-in consent. Do not try to sneak promotional content into a transactional message. Regulators know the difference, and so do your customers.</p>

<blockquote><p>Compliance is not a burden. It is the foundation of trust. Customers who opted in and know they can opt out are far more receptive than people who feel trapped on a list they never signed up for.</p></blockquote>

