<h2>Message structure and character limits</h2>

<p>A standard SMS is 160 characters. Go over that limit and your message gets split into multiple texts, which can arrive out of order, look messy, and cost you more to send. Keeping your messages under 160 characters is not just a best practice. It is a discipline that forces you to be clear and direct.</p>

<p>Every effective SMS follows a simple structure:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Lead with the value.</strong> What is in it for the reader? Put the benefit or the offer first. "20% off your next haircut" lands harder than "Hi, we wanted to let you know about a special offer we are running this week."</li>
  <li><strong>Include a clear call to action.</strong> Tell them exactly what to do next. "Book now: [link]" or "Reply YES to confirm" or "Show this text in store." One action, stated clearly.</li>
  <li><strong>Use the customer's name.</strong> Personalisation starts here. "[Name], your appointment is tomorrow at 2pm" feels personal. "Dear valued customer" does not.</li>
  <li><strong>Identify your business.</strong> Either use your business name as the sender ID or include it in the message. The recipient should never have to wonder who texted them.</li>
  <li><strong>Include the opt-out.</strong> "Reply STOP to opt out" at the end of every marketing message. This is both a legal requirement and a trust signal.</li>
</ul>

<p>Here is an example that hits all of these points in under 160 characters:</p>

<p><strong>"Hi Sarah, flash sale at Bloom Studio this Fri only. 25% off all facials. Book now: [link]. Reply STOP to opt out."</strong></p>

<p>That is 120 characters. Clear value, clear action, personalised, identified, and compliant. Everything a good SMS needs.</p>

<h2>Timing your campaigns for maximum impact</h2>

<p>When you send an SMS matters almost as much as what it says. A perfectly crafted message at 6am on a Sunday will annoy people. The same message at 11am on a Tuesday will get results.</p>

<p><strong>Best days.</strong> Tuesday through Thursday tend to perform best for promotional messages. Monday is too hectic. Friday afternoon, people are mentally checked out. Weekends can work for certain businesses (restaurants, entertainment, retail) but should be used carefully.</p>

<p><strong>Best times.</strong> Between 10am and 12pm is the sweet spot for most audiences. The second-best window is between 1pm and 3pm, after the lunch break. Avoid anything before 9am or after 8pm. Nobody wants to be woken up by a marketing text.</p>

<p><strong>Frequency.</strong> This is where most businesses go wrong. More is not better. For marketing SMS, two to four messages per month is the range that keeps you present without becoming annoying. If you are sending more than one marketing text per week, you will see your opt-out rate climb fast.</p>

<p>Transactional messages like appointment reminders do not count toward this frequency limit. Those are expected and welcomed by the customer. But your promotional campaigns need to be spaced out and each one needs to deliver genuine value.</p>

<blockquote><p>Every SMS you send is a withdrawal from your customer's attention bank. Make sure each one deposits enough value to keep the balance positive.</p></blockquote>

