<h2>Personalisation beyond the first name</h2>

<p>Inserting someone's first name into a message is the bare minimum of personalisation. It helps, but it is not enough to stand out. Real personalisation means sending the right message to the right person based on what you actually know about them.</p>

<p><strong>Segment by service type.</strong> A customer who books haircuts should not receive the same promotion as someone who comes in for facials. Tag your contacts by the services they use and create campaigns tailored to each group.</p>

<p><strong>Segment by purchase history.</strong> First-time customers need a different message than regulars. Someone who has visited five times is ready for a loyalty reward. Someone who came once three months ago needs a re-engagement campaign. Treating them the same wastes both opportunities.</p>

<p><strong>Segment by location.</strong> If your business operates in multiple areas, send location-specific offers. "Flash sale at our Bondi studio" is more relevant than a generic promotion for customers who only visit your Parramatta location.</p>

<p><strong>Segment by engagement level.</strong> Track who opens, clicks, and converts from your SMS campaigns. Your most engaged contacts are your best candidates for premium offers and early access. Less engaged contacts might need a different approach, or they might need to be moved to a less frequent cadence.</p>

<p>The point is simple: targeted messages outperform broadcast messages every single time. A campaign sent to 200 well-segmented contacts will generate more revenue than the same campaign sent to 2,000 unfiltered contacts. Segmentation is not extra work. It is the difference between SMS marketing that works and SMS marketing that gets ignored.</p>

<p>If you are already using a CRM or an <a href="/blog/email-marketing-small-business">email marketing platform</a> that tracks customer data, you likely have everything you need to start segmenting. Apply the same logic to your SMS campaigns and watch the results improve.</p>

<h2>Measuring your SMS ROI</h2>

<p>You cannot improve what you do not measure. SMS has the advantage of being a direct-response channel, which makes tracking relatively straightforward compared to brand awareness campaigns or <a href="/blog/social-media-scheduling">social media</a> efforts. Here are the metrics that matter.</p>

<p><strong>Delivery rate.</strong> The percentage of messages that successfully reach the recipient's phone. This should sit above 95%. If it is lower, you likely have outdated or incorrect numbers in your database. Clean your contact list regularly.</p>

<p><strong>Click-through rate (CTR).</strong> If your SMS includes a link, track how many people click it. Use shortened, trackable URLs so you can see exactly which campaign drove the traffic. A good CTR for promotional SMS is between 10% and 20%.</p>

<p><strong>Conversion rate.</strong> Of the people who clicked, how many completed the desired action? Did they book the appointment, redeem the offer, leave the review, or make the purchase? This is the metric that connects your SMS efforts directly to revenue.</p>

<p><strong>Opt-out rate.</strong> Monitor how many people unsubscribe after each campaign. A healthy opt-out rate is under 2% per send. If you see a spike, something went wrong. The message was not relevant, the timing was off, or you are sending too frequently. Treat opt-out spikes as diagnostic signals, not just numbers to accept.</p>

<p><strong>Revenue per message.</strong> Divide the revenue generated by a campaign by the number of messages sent. This gives you a clear, dollar-value picture of what each SMS is worth to your business. Over time, this number tells you exactly which campaign types and segments are most profitable.</p>

<p>The goal is to build a feedback loop. Send a campaign, measure the results, identify what worked, and apply those learnings to the next campaign. Over a few months, you will develop a clear picture of what your audience responds to, which segments convert best, and how much revenue your SMS channel is actually driving.</p>

